window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["pytorch-hands-on"]=[{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0001","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-001-1-pytorch-at-a-glance","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s002","q":"What is PyTorch's dynamic computation graph, and why does it make debugging easier?","a":"<p>PyTorch builds the graph while the operations run (define-by-run), so every forward pass can take a different path. Because the code is ordinary Python, you can use <code>print</code>, <code>pdb</code> or normal <code>if</code>/<code>for</code> logic inside a model and see real tensor values. The trade-off is that the framework cannot optimize the whole graph ahead of time, which is why <code>torch.compile</code> exists in PyTorch 2.x.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0002","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-001-1-pytorch-at-a-glance","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s002","q":"What are the four building blocks of PyTorch, and what does each do?","a":"<p>Tensors hold the data (n-dimensional arrays with GPU support). Autograd computes gradients automatically. The <code>torch.nn</code> / <code>torch.optim</code> / <code>torch.utils.data</code> API builds and trains models. The libraries (TorchVision, TorchAudio and others) add domain tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0003","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-001-1-pytorch-at-a-glance","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s002","q":"How is the PyTorch stack layered between Python, C++ and CUDA, and why is it built that way?","a":"<p>The Python API (<code>torch</code>, <code>torch.nn</code>, <code>torch.optim</code>, <code>torch.utils.data</code>) sits on a C++ core (ATen, the tensor library) which calls CUDA kernels on NVIDIA GPUs. You write easy Python, and the heavy math runs in compiled C++/CUDA, so you get Python productivity with near-native speed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0004","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-001-1-pytorch-at-a-glance","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s002","q":"How do you move a tensor to the GPU and change its data type?","a":"<p>Use <code>.to()</code>. <code>t.to('cuda')</code> moves the device and <code>t.to(dtype=torch.float32)</code> casts the type. Both return a new tensor and do not change the original in place. Inputs and the model must be on the same device or the op raises an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0005","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-001-1-pytorch-at-a-glance","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s002","q":"What does autograd do and why does a training loop need it?","a":"<p>Autograd is the automatic differentiation engine. It records operations and computes the gradient of the loss with respect to each parameter. The optimizer uses those gradients to move the weights in the direction that reduces the loss, so you never derive gradients by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0006","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-001-1-pytorch-at-a-glance","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s002","q":"How do you take a PyTorch model from prototype to production, and which tools are involved?","a":"<p>Prototype in eager mode, then optimize with <code>torch.compile</code> (PyTorch 2.x) or export. TorchScript (<code>torch.jit</code>) produces a static graph that runs without a Python runtime, and TorchServe serves models over REST. Version note: as of 2025, TorchScript is in maintenance mode in favor of <code>torch.export</code>, and TorchServe is in limited maintenance, so check current status before choosing them for new projects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0007","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-002-libraries","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s003","q":"Which <code>torch</code> submodule would you use for each job: layers and losses, optimizers, datasets and batching?","a":"<p>Layers, losses and activations are in <code>torch.nn</code> (for example <code>Linear</code>, <code>Conv2d</code>). Optimizers are in <code>torch.optim</code> (SGD, Adam, RMSprop). <code>Dataset</code> and <code>DataLoader</code> are in <code>torch.utils.data</code>. Differentiation lives in <code>torch.autograd</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0008","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-002-libraries","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s003","q":"In PyTorch 2.x, what is <code>torch.compile</code> and when would you use it?","a":"<p><code>torch.compile(model)</code> captures the model's operations and compiles them into optimized kernels (TorchDynamo plus the Inductor backend by default). Use it to speed up training or inference when the model runs many iterations with stable shapes. The first call is slower because of compilation, and frequent shape changes or Python branches can trigger recompiles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0009","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-002-libraries","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s003","q":"What is <code>torch.fx</code> used for?","a":"<p><code>torch.fx</code> is a toolkit for capturing a model as a graph of Python-level operations so you can inspect and transform it. Typical uses are fusing operations, quantization passes and custom analysis. It works on the traced program, not on data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0010","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-002-libraries","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s003","q":"What is <code>torch.distributed</code> for, and how does it differ from <code>torch.multiprocessing</code>?","a":"<p><code>torch.distributed</code> provides communication primitives and wrappers (such as DistributedDataParallel) for multi-GPU and multi-node training. <code>torch.multiprocessing</code> wraps Python's multiprocessing so tensors can be shared between processes, which is useful for parallel data loading and for launching worker processes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0011","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-002-libraries","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s003","q":"Why would a team use PyTorch Lightning or Hugging Face Transformers on top of PyTorch?","a":"<p>PyTorch Lightning removes training-loop boilerplate (device placement, checkpointing, distributed setup) while keeping plain PyTorch modules. Hugging Face Transformers supplies pretrained NLP and multimodal models with ready tokenizers. The trade-off is another abstraction layer and dependency to learn and keep in sync.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0012","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-002-libraries","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s003","q":"Which tool would you reach for to serve an LLM with high throughput, and which to train a very large model across many GPUs?","a":"<p>vLLM is built for high-throughput LLM inference (paged KV-cache and continuous batching). DeepSpeed (from Microsoft) targets large-scale distributed training with memory-saving techniques such as ZeRO.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0013","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-003-2-environment-setup","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s004","q":"How do you install the right PyTorch build for your machine?","a":"<p>Use the selector on pytorch.org to pick OS, package manager and compute platform (CPU or a CUDA version), then run the pip command it prints. Installing the wrong platform build is the usual cause of <code>torch.cuda.is_available()</code> returning <code>False</code>. On macOS the default build is CPU with Apple MPS support, not CUDA.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0014","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-003-2-environment-setup","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s004","q":"Why would you pin a version such as <code>pip3 install torch==2.2.0 torchvision</code>?","a":"<p>PyTorch's companion libraries (TorchVision, TorchAudio, TorchText) are released against specific PyTorch versions, and mismatches cause import or binary errors. Pinning keeps a project reproducible and lets you match a library that lags behind, such as TorchText, which has not been released beyond the PyTorch 2.3 era.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0015","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-003-2-environment-setup","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s004","q":"How do you quickly verify that PyTorch installed correctly and can use the GPU?","a":"<p>Run <code>import torch; print(torch.__version__)</code> to confirm the install, then <code>torch.cuda.is_available()</code> to confirm a usable CUDA GPU. On Apple Silicon check <code>torch.backends.mps.is_available()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0016","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-003-2-environment-setup","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s004","q":"Why should PyTorch projects live in a virtual environment?","a":"<p>PyTorch pulls in large binary dependencies and its companion libraries need matching versions. A virtual environment per project avoids conflicts between projects that need different torch versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0017","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-004-3-datasets-and-dataloaders","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s005","q":"What is the difference between <code>Dataset</code> and <code>DataLoader</code> in PyTorch, and why are they separate?","a":"<p>A <code>Dataset</code> defines how to get one sample and its label by index (<code>__getitem__</code>) and how many samples exist (<code>__len__</code>). A <code>DataLoader</code> wraps a dataset to give batching, shuffling and parallel loading with worker processes. Keeping them separate means you write the sample logic once and change batching or sampling strategy independently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0018","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-004-3-datasets-and-dataloaders","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s005","q":"What must you implement to write your own map-style <code>Dataset</code>?","a":"<p>Subclass <code>torch.utils.data.Dataset</code> and implement <code>__len__</code> (number of samples) and <code>__getitem__(idx)</code> (return one sample, usually a tensor and its label). <code>__init__</code> usually loads or indexes the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0019","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-004-3-datasets-and-dataloaders","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s005","q":"What do <code>batch_size</code>, <code>shuffle</code> and <code>num_workers</code> do in <code>DataLoader</code>?","a":"<p><code>batch_size</code> sets how many samples are stacked per step. <code>shuffle=True</code> reorders the data every epoch so batches differ, which you want for training but not for evaluation. <code>num_workers</code> sets how many subprocesses load data in parallel; 0 loads in the main process, which can starve the GPU.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0020","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-004-3-datasets-and-dataloaders","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s005","q":"Which dataset packages ship with PyTorch's domain libraries for vision, audio and text?","a":"<p><code>torchvision.datasets</code> has image sets such as FashionMNIST, CIFAR10 and ImageNet. <code>torchaudio.datasets</code> has audio sets such as LIBRISPEECH. <code>torchtext.datasets</code> has text sets such as IMDb and AG_NEWS. All return <code>Dataset</code> objects you can pass to a <code>DataLoader</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0021","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-005-fashionmnist-preloaded-with-train-valida","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s006","q":"In <code>torchvision.datasets.FashionMNIST(root=\"data\", train=True, download=True, transform=ToTensor())</code>, what do <code>train</code>, <code>download</code> and <code>transform</code> do?","a":"<p><code>train=True</code> selects the training split and <code>train=False</code> the test split. <code>download=True</code> fetches the data into <code>root</code> if it is not already there. <code>transform=ToTensor()</code> converts each PIL image to a float tensor scaled to [0, 1] with shape (C, H, W), so the <code>DataLoader</code> can batch it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0022","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-005-fashionmnist-preloaded-with-train-valida","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s006","q":"What does <code>torchvision.transforms.ToTensor</code> do to an image?","a":"<p>It converts a PIL image or NumPy array of shape (H, W, C) with uint8 values 0-255 into a <code>float32</code> tensor of shape (C, H, W) with values scaled to [0, 1]. The channel-first layout is what PyTorch convolution layers expect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0023","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-005-fashionmnist-preloaded-with-train-valida","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s006","q":"How do you carve a validation set out of a training dataset using <code>SubsetRandomSampler</code>, and why not use <code>shuffle=True</code> with it?","a":"<p>Shuffle a list of indices, split it into two index lists, and give each list to its own <code>DataLoader</code> through <code>sampler=SubsetRandomSampler(indices)</code>. The sampler already randomizes order within its subset. A <code>DataLoader</code> does not allow <code>shuffle=True</code> together with a custom <code>sampler</code>, because both would control the order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0024","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-005-fashionmnist-preloaded-with-train-valida","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s006","q":"Why should hyperparameter tuning use a validation split carved from the training data instead of the test set?","a":"<p>The test set must stay untouched until the end so it gives an unbiased estimate. You tune hyperparameters and decide when to stop on validation data taken from the training data. Using the test set for tuning leaks information and overstates real performance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0025","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-005-fashionmnist-preloaded-with-train-valida","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s006","q":"What is wrong with this validation split for an 80/20 train/validation ratio?","a":"<p>The slices are reversed. <code>indices[:split]</code> is only 20% of the data, so training would use 20% and validation 80%. Use <code>indices[split:]</code> for training and <code>indices[:split]</code> for validation. Always check slice direction when splitting by index.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>split = int(np.floor(0.2 * len(training_data)))\ntrain_sample = SubsetRandomSampler(indices[:split])\nvalidate_sample = SubsetRandomSampler(indices[split:])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0026","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-005-fashionmnist-preloaded-with-train-valida","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s006","q":"In a PyTorch loop, what does <code>images, labels = next(iter(trainloader))</code> give you?","a":"<p>It builds an iterator over the <code>DataLoader</code> and returns the first batch. <code>images</code> has shape (batch_size, C, H, W) and <code>labels</code> has shape (batch_size,). It is a quick way to check shapes and visualize data before training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0027","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-005-fashionmnist-preloaded-with-train-valida","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s006","q":"Why should the test <code>DataLoader</code> normally use <code>shuffle=False</code>?","a":"<p>Evaluation does not depend on order, and a fixed order makes results and per-sample error analysis reproducible. Shuffling only matters for training, where it reduces correlation between successive batches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0028","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-006-custom-csv-dataset-tutors","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s007","q":"How do you load numeric columns from a CSV file into a PyTorch tensor?","a":"<p>Read the file with <code>pandas.read_csv</code>, select the columns, and convert with <code>torch.tensor(df['col'].values)</code> or <code>torch.from_numpy(df['col'].to_numpy())</code>. The pipeline is CSV, then DataFrame, then NumPy array, then tensor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0029","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-006-custom-csv-dataset-tutors","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s007","q":"When you build a tensor from a pandas integer column with <code>torch.tensor(df['col'].values)</code>, what dtype do you get and why might that be a problem?","a":"<p>You get <code>int64</code>, because pandas stores integers as <code>int64</code> and PyTorch keeps that dtype. Model weights are <code>float32</code>, so feeding integer features into a <code>Linear</code> layer raises a dtype mismatch. Cast with <code>.float()</code> or <code>.to(torch.float32)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0030","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-006-custom-csv-dataset-tutors","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s007","q":"For a larger CSV, why wrap the data in a custom <code>Dataset</code> rather than keeping one big tensor?","a":"<p>A <code>Dataset</code> plugs into <code>DataLoader</code> for batching, shuffling and parallel loading, and it can load or transform rows lazily instead of holding everything in memory. It also keeps feature and label handling in one place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0031","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-006-custom-csv-dataset-tutors","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s007","q":"What does <code>pd.read_csv(path, nrows=50)</code> do and when is it useful?","a":"<p><code>nrows=50</code> reads only the first 50 data rows. It is useful for quickly inspecting the schema or testing a pipeline on a large file without loading it all.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0032","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-007-4-tensors-and-basic-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s008","q":"What is a tensor in PyTorch, and how does it differ from a NumPy array?","a":"<p>A tensor is an n-dimensional array like a NumPy <code>ndarray</code>, but it can live on a GPU and can track operations for autograd. NumPy arrays are CPU-only and have no gradient tracking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0033","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-007-4-tensors-and-basic-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s008","q":"What are the ranks of a scalar, vector, matrix and image batch, and what does each typically hold in deep learning?","a":"<p>A 0-D scalar holds a single value such as a loss. A 1-D vector holds features. A 2-D matrix holds tabular data or weights. A 3-D or higher tensor holds images or sequences; an image batch is usually 4-D (N, C, H, W).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0034","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-007-4-tensors-and-basic-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s008","q":"What is the difference between <code>torch.tensor(data)</code> and <code>torch.from_numpy(arr)</code>, and when do you prefer each?","a":"<p><code>torch.tensor</code> always copies the data, so later changes to the array cannot affect the tensor, and you can set <code>dtype</code> or <code>device</code> at creation. <code>torch.from_numpy</code> shares memory with the NumPy array. It is faster and uses no extra memory, but writes to one are visible in the other (CPU only).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0035","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-008-data-types","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s009","q":"What is the default float dtype in PyTorch, and why is it the default?","a":"<p>It is <code>torch.float32</code>. It offers enough precision for training neural networks while using half the memory and bandwidth of <code>float64</code>, and GPUs are optimized for it. <code>float64</code> is rarely needed in deep learning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0036","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-008-data-types","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s009","q":"Which dtype should classification labels have for <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code>?","a":"<p>Class-index targets must be <code>int64</code> (<code>torch.long</code>), with values from 0 to num_classes-1. The model outputs are <code>float32</code> logits. Passing float class indices raises an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0037","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-008-data-types","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s009","q":"What is the difference between <code>float16</code> and <code>bfloat16</code>, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Both use 16 bits. <code>float16</code> has more mantissa precision but a small range and needs loss scaling to avoid underflow. <code>bfloat16</code> keeps the same exponent range as <code>float32</code> with less precision, so it trains more stably without loss scaling. <code>bfloat16</code> needs hardware support such as TPUs and newer GPUs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0038","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-008-data-types","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s009","q":"What does this code return, and what is the effect of the second line?","a":"<p><code>a</code> is a 2x3 tensor of ones stored as <code>int16</code>. <code>a.to(dtype=torch.float32)</code> returns a new <code>float32</code> tensor; <code>a</code> itself is unchanged. <code>.to</code> is the standard way to cast type or move device.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>a = torch.ones((2, 3), dtype=torch.int16)\nb = a.to(dtype=torch.float32)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0039","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-008-data-types","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s009","q":"When would you use <code>uint8</code> or <code>int8</code> tensors?","a":"<p><code>uint8</code> is the usual storage type for raw image pixels (0-255), and <code>int8</code>/<code>uint8</code> are used in quantized models to cut memory and speed up inference at small accuracy cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0040","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-008-data-types","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s009","q":"What happens when you add an <code>int64</code> tensor to a <code>float32</code> tensor in PyTorch?","a":"<p>PyTorch applies type promotion and returns a <code>float32</code> result. Operations that need matching types, such as <code>matmul</code> between a float and an integer tensor, raise an error, so cast explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0041","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-009-size-and-dimensionality","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s010","q":"What is the difference between <code>.size()</code>, <code>.shape</code>, <code>.ndim</code> and <code>.ndimension()</code>?","a":"<p><code>.size()</code> and <code>.shape</code> both return a <code>torch.Size</code> with the length of each dimension. <code>.ndim</code> and <code>.ndimension()</code> both return the number of dimensions (the rank). They are equivalent pairs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0042","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-009-size-and-dimensionality","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s010","q":"When do you reshape a tensor, and how do <code>view</code> and <code>reshape</code> differ?","a":"<p>You reshape when a layer expects a specific input shape, for example flattening images to a vector before a <code>Linear</code> layer. <code>view</code> returns a tensor sharing the same memory and requires compatible contiguous memory, otherwise it raises an error. <code>reshape</code> returns a view when possible and copies when not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0043","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-009-size-and-dimensionality","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s010","q":"What does <code>-1</code> mean in <code>x.view(-1, 784)</code>?","a":"<p><code>-1</code> tells PyTorch to infer that dimension from the total number of elements. Here it infers the batch size, so a (64, 1, 28, 28) tensor becomes (64, 784).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0044","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-009-size-and-dimensionality","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s010","q":"What is the purpose of <code>unsqueeze(0)</code> and <code>squeeze()</code>?","a":"<p><code>unsqueeze(0)</code> adds a size-1 dimension at position 0, commonly to turn one image (C, H, W) into a batch of one (1, C, H, W). <code>squeeze()</code> removes size-1 dimensions, for example for plotting a (1, 28, 28) image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0045","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"How do arithmetic operations behave on tensors by default?","a":"<p>They are element-wise, so <code>u + v</code> adds matching positions. Shapes must be equal or broadcastable. Use <code>torch.matmul</code> or <code>@</code> for matrix multiplication.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0046","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"What does <code>torch.clamp(a, -0.5, 0.5)</code> do, and where is it used?","a":"<p>It limits every element to the range [-0.5, 0.5]: smaller values become -0.5 and larger ones 0.5. It is used for value bounds, for example gradient or probability clipping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0047","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"How do <code>torch.ceil</code> and <code>torch.floor</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>ceil</code> rounds each element up to the next integer and <code>floor</code> rounds down. For -0.3, <code>ceil</code> gives -0 (negative zero) and <code>floor</code> gives -1.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0048","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"What does <code>x.mean()</code> return, and how do you average along one dimension only?","a":"<p><code>x.mean()</code> reduces the whole tensor to a scalar tensor. <code>x.mean(dim=0)</code> averages along dimension 0 and returns a smaller tensor. <code>keepdim=True</code> keeps the reduced dimension with size 1.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0049","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"What is the difference between <code>torch.mul</code> / <code>*</code> and <code>torch.matmul</code> / <code>@</code>?","a":"<p><code>*</code> multiplies element by element (with broadcasting). <code>@</code> or <code>matmul</code> performs matrix multiplication, so the inner dimensions must match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0050","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"Why do <code>torch.sin(x)</code> results for <code>x = [0, pi/2, pi]</code> show tiny non-zero values instead of exactly 0 at pi?","a":"<p><code>float32</code> cannot represent pi exactly, so <code>sin(pi)</code> is about 1e-8 rather than 0. Compare floats with a tolerance, for example <code>torch.allclose</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0051","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"What does <code>torch.linspace(-np.pi/2, np.pi/2, steps=1000)</code> produce, and how does it differ from <code>torch.arange</code>?","a":"<p><code>linspace</code> produces exactly the requested number of evenly spaced points, including both endpoints. <code>arange</code> takes a step size and excludes the end, so the point count follows from the step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0052","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-010-operations","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s011","q":"Why do PyTorch tensor operations beat NumPy for deep learning?","a":"<p>They run on GPUs and support autograd, so the same expression can be differentiated and accelerated. NumPy runs on CPU with no gradient tracking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0053","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-011-broadcasting","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s012","q":"What are the broadcasting rules in PyTorch?","a":"<p>Compare shapes from the last dimension to the first. Each pair of sizes must be equal, or one of them must be 1, or one tensor must lack that dimension. A size-1 or missing dimension is stretched virtually to match. Otherwise PyTorch raises an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0054","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-011-broadcasting","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s012","q":"What is the shape of <code>torch.ones(5, 1) + torch.ones(1, 4)</code>?","a":"<p>(5, 4). The size-1 dimensions stretch, so each of the 5 rows combines with each of the 4 columns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0055","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-011-broadcasting","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s012","q":"Why is broadcasting memory efficient?","a":"<p>It does not make physical copies of the smaller tensor. The size-1 dimension is treated as repeated through a stride of zero, so applying a weight or bias across a batch needs no extra memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0056","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-011-broadcasting","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s012","q":"Will <code>torch.ones(4, 3, 2) * torch.rand(3, 1)</code> work, and what shape results?","a":"<p>Yes, the result is (4, 3, 2). Aligned from the right, (3, 1) matches (3, 2) because the last size is 1 and stretches, and the missing leading dimension is treated as 1.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0057","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-011-broadcasting","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s012","q":"What error do you get from <code>torch.ones(3, 4) + torch.ones(2, 4)</code> and why?","a":"<p>A runtime error about sizes that must match at dimension 0. Sizes 3 and 2 are neither equal nor 1, so the shapes are not broadcastable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0058","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-011-broadcasting","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s012","q":"What is a common bug caused by silent broadcasting in a loss function?","a":"<p>If predictions have shape (N, 1) and targets have shape (N,), subtracting them broadcasts to (N, N) and gives a wrong loss without any error. Make the shapes match with <code>squeeze</code> or <code>view</code> before computing the loss.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0059","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-012-pytorch-numpy-bridge","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s013","q":"In PyTorch, what happens to a NumPy array when you change the tensor made by <code>torch.from_numpy(arr)</code>?","a":"<p>The array changes too, because both share the same memory buffer (CPU only). Conversions in both directions are zero-copy. To decouple them, use <code>.clone()</code> on the tensor or <code>.copy()</code> on the array.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0060","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-012-pytorch-numpy-bridge","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s013","q":"How do you convert a tensor that requires grad, or lives on the GPU, to NumPy?","a":"<p>Calling <code>.numpy()</code> on such a tensor fails. Detach and move to CPU first: <code>t.detach().cpu().numpy()</code>. NumPy cannot hold GPU memory or gradient tracking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0061","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-012-pytorch-numpy-bridge","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s013","q":"What dtype does a tensor from <code>torch.from_numpy(np.ones((2, 3)))</code> have, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>It is <code>float64</code>, since NumPy defaults to 64-bit floats. Model weights are <code>float32</code>, so convert with <code>.float()</code> before feeding the model to avoid a dtype mismatch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0062","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"What are the three steps of one training iteration in PyTorch, and which autograd function takes part?","a":"<p>Forward pass: compute predictions and the loss. Backward pass: <code>loss.backward()</code> computes the gradients with the chain rule. Optimization: <code>optimizer.step()</code> updates the weights. Call <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code> before the backward pass so old gradients do not add in.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0063","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"What does <code>requires_grad=True</code> do on a tensor, and what is a leaf tensor?","a":"<p>It tells autograd to track operations on the tensor and store its gradient in <code>.grad</code>. A leaf tensor is one you created directly (not the result of an operation), such as a parameter. After <code>backward()</code>, only leaf tensors with <code>requires_grad=True</code> keep their <code>.grad</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0064","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"What does <code>grad_fn</code> hold?","a":"<p>The function that created a tensor, such as <code>AddBackward0</code>. It links the tensor into the graph so autograd knows how to push gradients backward. Leaf tensors have <code>grad_fn=None</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0065","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"What value does <code>x.grad</code> have here, and how is it computed?","a":"<p><code>tensor([7.])</code>. The derivative is dy/dx = 2x + 3, which is 7 at x = 2. <code>backward()</code> walks the graph from y back to x using the chain rule.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = torch.tensor([2.0], requires_grad=True)\ny = x**2 + 3*x\ny.backward()\nprint(x.grad)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0066","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"Why does <code>y.backward()</code> work without arguments only when <code>y</code> is a scalar?","a":"<p>A gradient of a scalar with respect to inputs is well defined. For a non-scalar <code>y</code> you must pass a <code>gradient</code> argument (the vector in a vector-Jacobian product), for example <code>y.backward(torch.ones_like(y))</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0067","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"Why does PyTorch free the graph after <code>backward()</code>, and how do you backpropagate twice through it?","a":"<p>Buffers saved for the backward pass are released to save memory. Calling <code>backward()</code> a second time on the same graph raises an error. Pass <code>retain_graph=True</code> on the first call if you need another pass, at the cost of keeping memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0068","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"How does a dynamic graph help with models such as RNNs or transformers with conditional logic?","a":"<p>The graph is rebuilt on every forward pass, so loops and <code>if</code> statements that depend on data or sequence length work naturally, and autograd records whatever path actually ran.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0069","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-013-6-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s014","q":"In <code>from torch.autograd import Variable</code>, what is <code>Variable</code> and should you use it?","a":"<p><code>Variable</code> is a legacy wrapper from before PyTorch 0.4. Since 0.4, tensors track gradients themselves, and <code>Variable(t)</code> simply returns a tensor. Do not use it in new code; use <code>requires_grad=True</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0070","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-014-visualizing-the-graph-with-torchviz","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s015","q":"What does <code>torchviz.make_dot</code> do and what does it need?","a":"<p><code>make_dot(z, params={...})</code> builds a Graphviz diagram of the autograd graph that produced tensor <code>z</code>, with nodes for operations and for leaf tensors. It needs the <code>torchviz</code> Python package and the Graphviz system binary installed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0071","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-014-visualizing-the-graph-with-torchviz","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s015","q":"Given a = 2, b = 3, x = a*b, y = x + b, z = y**2, what are dz/da and dz/db?","a":"<p>Here y = a*b + b = 9, so z = y**2. dz/da = 2y * b = 2*9*3 = 54. dz/db = 2y * (a + 1) = 2*9*3 = 54, because b reaches z through both x and the direct addition, so the two paths add up. Both gradients equal 54 for these values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0072","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-014-visualizing-the-graph-with-torchviz","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s015","q":"Why use a graph visualization when debugging gradients?","a":"<p>It shows which operations produced a tensor and whether a tensor is attached to the graph. A missing connection, for example after <code>.detach()</code> or <code>.item()</code>, explains why a parameter gets no gradient.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0073","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-015-practical-rules","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s016","q":"Why use <code>with torch.no_grad():</code> during inference?","a":"<p>It stops autograd from recording operations, so no graph is stored. That saves memory and time. It does not change layer behavior such as dropout or batch norm; call <code>model.eval()</code> for that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0074","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-015-practical-rules","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s016","q":"What is the difference between <code>torch.no_grad()</code> and <code>torch.inference_mode()</code>?","a":"<p>Both disable gradient tracking. <code>inference_mode</code> is stricter and faster: tensors created in it are inference tensors that cannot later be used in autograd. Use it for pure inference; use <code>no_grad</code> if the results may be used for autograd later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0075","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-015-practical-rules","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s016","q":"What does <code>.detach()</code> do and when do you use it?","a":"<p>It returns a tensor sharing the same data but cut from the graph, so no gradient flows back through it. Use it to stop gradients through a target or to log values and convert to NumPy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0076","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-015-practical-rules","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s016","q":"Why must you call <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code> in each iteration?","a":"<p>PyTorch accumulates gradients into <code>.grad</code> on every <code>backward()</code>. Without clearing them, gradients of earlier batches add to the current one and updates are wrong. Accumulation can also be used on purpose to simulate larger batches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0077","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-015-practical-rules","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s016","q":"What is the correct order of <code>zero_grad</code>, <code>backward</code> and <code>step</code> in a training loop?","a":"<p>Clear gradients (<code>zero_grad</code>), compute the loss and call <code>loss.backward()</code>, then <code>optimizer.step()</code>. <code>step</code> must come after <code>backward</code>, which fills <code>.grad</code>. <code>zero_grad</code> must run before the next <code>backward</code>, and it can go at the start or right after <code>step</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0078","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-015-practical-rules","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s016","q":"How does <code>model.eval()</code> differ from <code>torch.no_grad()</code>?","a":"<p><code>model.eval()</code> switches layers such as dropout and batch norm to inference behavior. <code>no_grad</code> turns off gradient tracking. For evaluation you normally use both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0079","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"How does <code>torch.autograd.grad(y, x)</code> differ from <code>y.backward()</code>?","a":"<p><code>grad</code> returns the gradients as a tuple and does not write to <code>.grad</code>. <code>backward</code> accumulates results into the <code>.grad</code> of leaf tensors. <code>grad</code> gives finer control and is the basis for higher-order derivatives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0080","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"How do you compute a second derivative in PyTorch?","a":"<p>Compute the first derivative with <code>torch.autograd.grad(y, x, create_graph=True)</code>, then differentiate that result again. <code>create_graph=True</code> keeps the graph of the derivative computation, otherwise the first gradient is a constant with no graph.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0081","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"What is wrong with this second-derivative code?","a":"<p>The first call lacks <code>create_graph=True</code>, so <code>dy_dx</code> has no graph and the second call raises an error saying the tensor does not require grad. Add <code>create_graph=True</code> to the first call.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = torch.tensor([2.0], requires_grad=True)\ny = x**3\ndy_dx = torch.autograd.grad(y, x)[0]\nd2y_dx2 = torch.autograd.grad(dy_dx, x)[0]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0082","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"How do you write a custom autograd operation with <code>torch.autograd.Function</code>?","a":"<p>Subclass <code>Function</code> and define two static methods: <code>forward(ctx, ...)</code> computes the output, and <code>backward(ctx, grad_output)</code> returns the gradient with respect to each input. Call it through <code>.apply</code>. Use it for operations without built-in gradients or for custom gradient rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0083","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"In a custom <code>torch.autograd.Function</code>, what is <code>ctx.save_for_backward</code> for?","a":"<p>It stores tensors from the forward pass so the backward pass can read them as <code>ctx.saved_tensors</code>. Using it, rather than plain attributes, lets autograd check that saved tensors were not modified in place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0084","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"What should <code>backward</code> return for <code>forward</code> computing <code>input ** 2</code>?","a":"<p><code>grad_output * 2 * input</code>. This is the chain rule: the incoming gradient times the local derivative. It needs <code>input</code> saved in forward.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0085","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"What are the Jacobian and the Hessian?","a":"<p>The Jacobian is the matrix of first partial derivatives of a vector-valued function. The Hessian is the matrix of second partial derivatives of a scalar function. In PyTorch use <code>torch.autograd.functional.jacobian</code> and <code>hessian</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0086","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"What is the Hessian of f(x) = x0<strong>2 + x1</strong>3 at (1, 2)?","a":"<p>A diagonal matrix: <code>[[2, 0], [0, 12]]</code>. The second derivative in x0 is 2, in x1 it is 6*x1 = 12, and the cross terms are 0.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0087","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"What is <code>register_hook</code> on a tensor used for?","a":"<p>It registers a function called with the tensor's gradient during the backward pass. Use it to inspect gradients, log them, or modify them by returning a new gradient (for example clipping).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0088","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-016-7-advanced-autograd","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s017","q":"When would you use higher-order gradients in practice?","a":"<p>Examples are Newton-style optimizers, gradient penalties such as in WGAN-GP, and meta-learning such as MAML. They cost more memory because the derivative graph is kept.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0089","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-017-8-torchvision","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s018","q":"What are the main components of TorchVision?","a":"<p><code>datasets</code> (CIFAR, ImageNet, COCO), <code>io</code> (read and write images and video), <code>models</code> (classification, detection and segmentation architectures, often with pretrained weights), <code>ops</code> (such as non-maximum suppression), <code>transforms</code> (image preprocessing and augmentation) and <code>utils</code> (such as <code>make_grid</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0090","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-017-8-torchvision","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s018","q":"Why use a pretrained model from <code>torchvision.models</code> instead of training from scratch?","a":"<p>Pretrained weights learned general visual features on a large dataset, so you need less data and compute and usually get better results. You freeze or fine-tune the backbone and replace the final layer for your classes. Input must match the preprocessing the weights were trained with.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0091","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-017-8-torchvision","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s018","q":"What is non-maximum suppression and which TorchVision function applies it?","a":"<p>NMS removes overlapping detection boxes for the same object, keeping the highest-scoring one. <code>torchvision.ops.nms</code> does this and <code>batched_nms</code> does it per class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0092","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-017-8-torchvision","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s018","q":"Which TorchVision model families fit classification, detection and segmentation?","a":"<p>Classification: ResNet, EfficientNet, VGG, MobileNet. Detection: Faster R-CNN, Mask R-CNN, RetinaNet. Segmentation: DeepLab.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0093","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-017-8-torchvision","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s018","q":"What does <code>torchvision.utils.make_grid</code> do?","a":"<p>It arranges a batch of image tensors into one grid image, which is handy for visualizing a batch or augmentation results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0094","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-017-8-torchvision","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s018","q":"What is <code>torchvision.io.read_image</code> and how does it differ from <code>PIL.Image.open</code>?","a":"<p><code>read_image</code> decodes JPEG or PNG straight into a <code>uint8</code> tensor of shape (C, H, W). <code>Image.open</code> returns a PIL image object (H, W, C when converted). The tensor works directly with tensor transforms and the GPU.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0095","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-018-lab-image-captioning-blip-and-embeddings","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s019","q":"What does BLIP do versus CLIP?","a":"<p>BLIP is a vision-language model that generates a text caption for an image. CLIP is trained contrastively on image-text pairs and maps images and text into a shared embedding space, so it supports similarity, zero-shot classification and retrieval but does not write captions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0096","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-018-lab-image-captioning-blip-and-embeddings","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s019","q":"In Hugging Face Transformers, why is a <code>BlipProcessor</code> needed alongside <code>BlipForConditionalGeneration</code>?","a":"<p>The processor holds the image preprocessing (resize, normalize) and the tokenizer for the model. It turns a PIL image into the <code>pixel_values</code> tensor the model expects and decodes output token IDs back to text. The model alone cannot take raw images.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0097","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-018-lab-image-captioning-blip-and-embeddings","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s019","q":"How would you build similar-image search with CLIP image embeddings?","a":"<p>Compute an embedding for each image with <code>get_image_features</code>, normalize it, and store the vectors in an index. For a query image or text, compute its embedding and run nearest-neighbor search with cosine similarity. For large collections use an approximate nearest-neighbor index such as FAISS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0098","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-018-lab-image-captioning-blip-and-embeddings","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s019","q":"Why does the image have to go through <code>.convert(\"RGB\")</code> before preprocessing?","a":"<p>Images can be grayscale, RGBA or palette mode. Converting to RGB guarantees 3 channels, which the model's preprocessing expects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0099","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"How do <code>transforms.Compose</code> and <code>torch.nn.Sequential</code> differ for chaining image transforms in TorchVision?","a":"<p><code>Compose</code> chains transforms on PIL images or tensors and is the classic choice. <code>nn.Sequential</code> works only with tensor-compatible transforms but runs them on tensors (including GPU) and can be scripted with TorchScript. Both apply transforms in order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0100","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"Why use random transforms like <code>RandomCrop</code> and <code>RandomHorizontalFlip</code> in training?","a":"<p>They are data augmentation. Each call gives a different variation, so the model sees more varied inputs and overfits less. Apply them to training data only; evaluation should use deterministic transforms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0101","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"In TorchVision, what does <code>T.RandomHorizontalFlip(p=0.3)</code> do?","a":"<p>It flips the image left to right with probability 0.3 and leaves it unchanged otherwise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0102","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"What is the difference between <code>RandomCrop(224)</code> and <code>CenterCrop(224)</code>?","a":"<p><code>RandomCrop</code> takes a crop at a random position each time, which is augmentation. <code>CenterCrop</code> always takes the center, giving deterministic output suited for validation and test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0103","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"What do <code>brightness=.5, hue=.3</code> do in <code>T.ColorJitter(brightness=.5, hue=.3)</code>?","a":"<p>Brightness is randomly scaled by a factor chosen from [0.5, 1.5], and hue is shifted by a random amount in [-0.3, 0.3]. Hue must be within [-0.5, 0.5].</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0104","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"What does <code>T.RandomPerspective(distortion_scale=0.6, p=1.0)</code> do?","a":"<p>It applies a random perspective warp with strength 0.6 and applies it with probability 1, so every image is warped. Higher <code>distortion_scale</code> means a stronger distortion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0105","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"What does <code>T.Pad(padding=10)</code> do, and what are the padding modes?","a":"<p>It adds a 10-pixel border on all sides. By default it fills with a constant (0, black). The <code>padding_mode</code> can also be <code>edge</code>, <code>reflect</code> or <code>symmetric</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0106","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"What does this code produce, and what pitfall appears when you apply a random transform separately to an image and to its segmentation mask?","a":"<p>It produces 4 different jittered copies, because random parameters are sampled on every call. The pitfall is the same: calling a random transform separately on an image and on its mask or label gives different results. For segmentation, use paired transforms, or the <code>torchvision.transforms.v2</code> API, which handles them together.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jitter = T.ColorJitter(brightness=.5, hue=.3)\nimgs = [jitter(orig_img) for _ in range(4)]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0107","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-019-lab-image-transforms","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s020","q":"Why would you apply image transforms on the GPU, and what is required?","a":"<p>Large augmentations are faster on the GPU and avoid CPU bottlenecks. Use tensor transforms in <code>nn.Sequential</code> and move the image tensor to the device first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0108","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-020-9-torchaudio","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s021","q":"What are the main parts of TorchAudio?","a":"<p><code>datasets</code> (LibriSpeech, Common Voice, Speech Commands), <code>io</code> (<code>load</code>, <code>save</code>, <code>info</code>), <code>transforms</code> (<code>nn.Module</code> classes such as <code>MelSpectrogram</code>, <code>MFCC</code>, <code>Resample</code>), <code>functional</code> (stateless functions), <code>pipelines</code> (ready-made models with preprocessing) and <code>models</code> (Wav2Vec2, HuBERT, Conformer, Tacotron2).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0109","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-020-9-torchaudio","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s021","q":"What is the difference between <code>torchaudio.transforms</code> and <code>torchaudio.functional</code>?","a":"<p><code>transforms</code> are <code>nn.Module</code> classes that hold parameters such as a filter bank, so you create them once and call them (and can place them in <code>nn.Sequential</code>). <code>functional</code> holds stateless functions where you pass all arguments on every call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0110","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-020-9-torchaudio","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s021","q":"What does <code>torchaudio.load(\"file.wav\")</code> return?","a":"<p>A tuple <code>(waveform, sample_rate)</code>. The waveform has shape (channels, time) and values are floats in [-1, 1] by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0111","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-020-9-torchaudio","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s021","q":"What is a Mel spectrogram and why do speech models use it?","a":"<p>It is a time-frequency representation where the frequency axis follows the Mel scale, which approximates human hearing. It compresses the raw waveform into a compact feature that speech and audio models learn from more easily.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0112","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-020-9-torchaudio","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s021","q":"Which TorchAudio transforms are used for audio augmentation?","a":"<p><code>TimeMasking</code> and <code>FrequencyMasking</code> (SpecAugment-style masking of spectrograms), <code>TimeStretch</code>, and <code>Resample</code> or speed perturbation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0113","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-020-9-torchaudio","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s021","q":"What does a TorchAudio pipeline bundle contain?","a":"<p>It bundles the pretrained model weights with the information needed to use them: sample rate, labels, and methods to build the model. Example: <code>torchaudio.pipelines.WAV2VEC2_ASR_BASE_960H</code>. This avoids mismatched preprocessing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0114","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-021-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"Why must audio be resampled to <code>bundle.sample_rate</code> before running a Wav2Vec2 model?","a":"<p>The model was trained on audio at a fixed rate (16 kHz for the standard bundles). Different rates change the time scale of the signal and degrade or break recognition. Use <code>torchaudio.functional.resample</code> first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0115","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-021-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"What are the shapes of <code>waveform</code> and the resulting <code>emission</code> in a Wav2Vec2 ASR flow?","a":"<p><code>waveform</code> is (channels, samples). <code>emission</code> is (batch, frames, num_labels), the per-frame scores for each character label, with one frame per roughly 20 ms of audio.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0116","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-021-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"What does <code>model.extract_features(waveform)</code> return for a Wav2Vec2 model?","a":"<p>A list with one feature tensor per transformer layer (12 for the base model) and the output lengths. Later layers carry more phonetic and linguistic information, so people inspect or take features from specific layers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0117","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-021-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"What does the TorchAudio backend setting control, and how do you handle it across versions?","a":"<p>The backend decides which library decodes audio files (for example <code>soundfile</code> or <code>sox_io</code>). Version note: in TorchAudio 2.x, <code>set_audio_backend</code> and <code>get_audio_backend</code> are deprecated and dispatch is automatic, with a <code>backend</code> argument on <code>torchaudio.load</code>. In TorchAudio 2.9 and later the backend-selection APIs (including <code>list_audio_backends</code>) are removed and loading goes through TorchCodec, so check the docs for your installed version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0118","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-021-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"Why would you set <code>ssl._create_default_https_context = ssl._create_unverified_context</code> when a model download fails?","a":"<p>It disables HTTPS certificate verification, which hides a certificate error but removes protection from man-in-the-middle attacks. Prefer fixing the certificate store (for example installing the CA certificates) and never use it in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0119","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-021-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"Why move the model and the waveform to the same device before inference?","a":"<p>Operations require tensors on one device. A model on the GPU with a CPU waveform raises a device mismatch error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0120","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"How does greedy CTC decoding work?","a":"<p>Take the argmax label for each frame, collapse consecutive repeated labels, then remove the blank token. The result is the transcript. It is fast and needs no language model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0121","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"Why does CTC need a blank token?","a":"<p>The blank separates repeated characters and marks frames with no output. Without it the decoder could not tell \"ll\" in \"hello\" from one \"l\" held across several frames. CTC also lets frames and characters align without labeled timing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0122","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"What does this decoding step compute?","a":"<p><code>argmax</code> picks the best label per frame, <code>unique_consecutive</code> collapses runs of the same label into one, and the list comprehension drops the blank (index 0 here). Order matters: collapse first, then remove blanks, so repeats separated by a blank are kept.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>indices = torch.argmax(emission, dim=-1)\nindices = torch.unique_consecutive(indices, dim=-1)\nindices = [i for i in indices if i != 0]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0123","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"What is the trade-off of greedy CTC decoding versus beam search with a language model?","a":"<p>Greedy is simple and fast but picks locally best labels, so it can produce misspellings and cannot use word knowledge. Beam search with a language model or lexicon is slower but usually gives lower word error rate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0124","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"How does Whisper differ from Wav2Vec2 with CTC for transcription?","a":"<p>Whisper is an encoder-decoder transformer trained on large multilingual supervised data, producing text directly with punctuation and handling many languages and translation. Wav2Vec2 with CTC outputs per-frame characters and needs a decoder. Whisper is heavier and slower; the CTC model is lighter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0125","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"What input does Whisper need?","a":"<p>16 kHz mono audio. Resample to 16 kHz, average channels to mono (<code>waveform.mean(dim=0, keepdim=True)</code>), and let <code>WhisperProcessor</code> compute log-Mel <code>input_features</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0126","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"What does <code>processor.batch_decode(predicted_ids, skip_special_tokens=True)[0]</code> do in a Hugging Face Whisper flow?","a":"<p>It converts the batch of predicted token IDs back to text strings. <code>skip_special_tokens=True</code> drops markers such as start, end, padding and language tokens, so you get clean text, and <code>[0]</code> picks the first item in the batch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0127","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-022-lab-speech-to-text","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s022","q":"How would you measure the quality of a speech-to-text system?","a":"<p>Use word error rate (WER): substitutions plus deletions plus insertions divided by the number of reference words. Character error rate is used for languages without clear word boundaries. Evaluate on audio that matches your real accents and noise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0128","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-023-10-torchtext","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s023","q":"What are the main components of TorchText, and what do they do?","a":"<p>Datasets (AG_NEWS, IMDB, WikiText), transforms (<code>VocabTransform</code>, <code>Truncate</code>, <code>PadTransform</code>, <code>ToTensor</code>, tokenizers), pretrained embeddings (GloVe, FastText, CharNGram) and vocabularies mapping tokens to indices. Output feeds <code>DataLoader</code> and <code>nn.Embedding</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0129","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-023-10-torchtext","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s023","q":"What is the TorchText maintenance status, and how does it affect its use?","a":"<p>Version note: TorchText is no longer developed; the last release was 0.18, matching PyTorch 2.3, and it is not published for newer PyTorch versions. For new projects use Hugging Face tokenizers/datasets or your own preprocessing, and pin matching versions if you must use it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0130","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-023-10-torchtext","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s023","q":"What does a vocabulary do in an NLP pipeline, and how does it relate to <code>nn.Embedding</code>?","a":"<p>A vocabulary maps each token to an integer index. <code>nn.Embedding(num_tokens, dim)</code> is a lookup table turning those indices into dense vectors. The vocabulary size must equal the number of rows in the embedding table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0131","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-023-10-torchtext","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s023","q":"Why do text pipelines need padding and truncation before batching?","a":"<p>Tensors in a batch must have equal length. Truncation caps long texts and padding fills short ones with a pad index, often with an attention or padding mask so the model ignores pad positions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0132","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-023-10-torchtext","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s023","q":"What are the differences between word, character and subword tokenization?","a":"<p>Word tokenization has a big vocabulary and out-of-vocabulary problems. Character tokenization has a tiny vocabulary but long sequences. Subword tokenization (BPE, SentencePiece) balances both, handles rare words by splitting them, and is what modern LLMs use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0133","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-023-10-torchtext","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s023","q":"What are GloVe and FastText embeddings, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>Both are pretrained static word vectors. GloVe is trained on global word co-occurrence counts. FastText represents words as bags of character n-grams, so it can build vectors for unseen words. Neither changes by context, unlike transformer embeddings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0134","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-024-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"In TorchText, why is <code>get_tokenizer(\"basic_english\")</code> used before looking up GloVe vectors?","a":"<p>It lowercases the text and splits it into tokens that match GloVe's vocabulary, which is lowercase. Tokens that do not match would have no vector.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0135","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-024-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"What does <code>GloVe(name='6B', dim=100)</code> select?","a":"<p>The GloVe vectors trained on a 6-billion-token corpus, with 100-dimensional vectors. Other options are other corpora (42B, 840B) and other dimensions (50, 200, 300). Higher dimension means more capacity and memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0136","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-024-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"Why filter tokens with <code>if token in glove.stoi</code> before building the embedding?","a":"<p>Out-of-vocabulary tokens would return zero vectors (or fail) and dilute the average. Filtering keeps only tokens with real vectors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0137","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-024-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"What does this code compute, and why the <code>else</code> branch?","a":"<p>It stacks the token vectors into a (num_tokens, 100) tensor and mean-pools over tokens, giving one fixed 100-d vector for text of any length. The <code>else</code> avoids a <code>stack</code> error on an empty list and returns a zero vector as a fallback.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if token_vectors:\n    session_embedding = torch.stack(token_vectors).mean(dim=0)\nelse:\n    session_embedding = torch.zeros(100)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0138","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-024-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"What are the limits of mean-pooled GloVe vectors as a sentence representation?","a":"<p>They ignore word order and context, treat every word equally (stop words add noise), and cannot separate senses of a word. For stronger semantics use sentence-transformer or transformer embeddings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0139","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-024-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"Why does using <code>torch.stack</code> rather than <code>torch.cat</code> fit mean-pooling a list of vectors?","a":"<p><code>stack</code> joins vectors along a new dimension, giving (num_tokens, 100), so <code>mean(dim=0)</code> averages across tokens. <code>cat</code> would join them into one long 1-D vector of length num_tokens*100.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0140","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-025-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"In Hugging Face <code>generate</code>, what do <code>num_beams</code>, <code>no_repeat_ngram_size</code>, <code>early_stopping</code> and <code>max_new_tokens</code> do?","a":"<p><code>num_beams</code> sets how many candidate sequences beam search keeps. <code>no_repeat_ngram_size=2</code> forbids any bigram from appearing twice. <code>early_stopping=True</code> stops once all beams finish. <code>max_new_tokens</code> caps how many tokens are added after the prompt, not counting the prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0141","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-025-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"What is the difference between greedy search, beam search and sampling for text generation?","a":"<p>Greedy picks the most likely token each step and can be repetitive. Beam search keeps several candidate sequences, producing more coherent, deterministic text at higher cost and with a tendency to be generic. Sampling draws from the distribution and gives diverse, less predictable output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0142","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-025-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"What do <code>temperature</code>, <code>top_k</code> and <code>top_p</code> do when <code>do_sample=True</code>?","a":"<p><code>temperature</code> rescales logits: under 1 sharpens the distribution (safer), over 1 flattens it (more random). <code>top_k</code> samples only from the k most likely tokens. <code>top_p</code> (nucleus sampling) samples from the smallest set of tokens whose probability adds up to p. Without <code>do_sample=True</code> these are ignored under greedy decoding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0143","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-025-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"When should you choose beam search over sampling?","a":"<p>Choose beam search for tasks with a narrow correct answer, such as translation or summarization. Choose sampling for open-ended or creative text where variety matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0144","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-025-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"Why is base GPT-2 a weak choice for following instructions like \"suggest exercises\"?","a":"<p>GPT-2 is a base language model trained only to continue text. It was not instruction-tuned, so it often continues the prompt instead of following it. Use an instruction-tuned model for such tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"pytorch-hands-on-f0145","card":"q-pytorch-hands-on-025-lab-glove-preprocessing-gpt-2-generation","sec":"pytorch-hands-on-s024","q":"In Hugging Face Transformers, what does <code>**inputs</code> do in <code>model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens=100)</code> when <code>inputs</code> is the dict returned by a tokenizer called with <code>return_tensors=\"pt\"</code>?","a":"<p>It unpacks the tokenizer's output dictionary (<code>input_ids</code>, <code>attention_mask</code>) into keyword arguments, so the model receives all of them without listing each.</p>","hand":true}];
